# Supabase — the publishable key is embedded in the client bundle by design.
# It is not a secret: Row-Level Security is what protects the data. Every table
# must have RLS enabled in the same migration that creates it.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Which ways in the app offers is asked of the project itself, at
# GET /auth/v1/settings — so enabling Spotify or email in the Supabase dashboard
# changes the sign-in page with no redeploy. Web3 is the exception: that endpoint
# reports no web3 field at all, so the chains are named here instead.
#
# Leave it unset for the default, which is both `solana` and `ethereum`. A chain
# is only ever offered when the browser also has a wallet for it, so the default
# is invisible to everyone without an extension installed.
#
# Setting it replaces the default rather than adding to it:
#   VITE_SUPABASE_WEB3_CHAINS=solana    # Solana only
#   VITE_SUPABASE_WEB3_CHAINS=          # no wallet sign-in at all
#
# That empty form is the only way to turn wallets off — nothing on the server
# says whether a project accepts them.

# PostHog browser analytics. Both must be set or analytics is off entirely:
# src/lib/posthog.ts leaves the client undefined and every capture is a no-op.
#
# The host is the managed reverse proxy this organization owns in front of
# PostHog EU, so the requests go to a first-party domain and a content blocker
# does not drop them. `https://eu.i.posthog.com` still works and is what a fork
# without a proxy wants. The PostHog app host is separate and stays hardcoded in
# src/lib/posthog.ts as `ui_host`.
VITE_POSTHOG_KEY=phc_your_project_token
VITE_POSTHOG_HOST=https://events.kikouchou.app

# Capture from a dev server. Leave this unset.
#
# With a key in .env.local, every load of http://localhost:… would otherwise
# create a real anonymous Person in the real project — that is how it came to
# hold 20 people against 3 Supabase accounts. src/lib/posthog.ts refuses to
# initialise on localhost unless this is exactly 'true'. Turn it on for the one
# session where you need to watch events arrive, then turn it off again.
# VITE_POSTHOG_ALLOW_LOCALHOST=true

# The link preview service, `server/share-preview`, answering on
# share.kikouchou.app. Set it and the share dialog hands out
# https://share.kikouchou.app/<lang>/<token>, which shows that trip's own name,
# dates and occupancy grid when the link is pasted into a chat, then redirects
# into the app.
#
# Leave it unset and the dialog hands out the direct
# https://app.kikouchou.app/join/<token> link instead. Both work; without the
# service every share shows the one generic card in index.html.
VITE_SHARE_ORIGIN=https://share.kikouchou.app

# Trip reminders over Web Push. The public half of the VAPID key pair (the keys
# that identify the push sender). Set it and a shared trip's calendar offers
# "Turn on reminders"; leave it unset and nothing about reminders is shown.
#
# Generate both halves with `node scripts/generate-vapid-keys.mjs`. The private
# half goes to the share-preview service below and nowhere else.
#
# The deployed build reads it from the `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` repository secret;
# see the Build step in .github/workflows/deploy.yml, which warns when it is
# missing rather than shipping a silently reminder-less app.
VITE_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=

# The preview service's own configuration — NOT read by the app. Nothing here is
# VITE_-prefixed, so Vite never puts any of it in the browser bundle. These are
# read by docker-compose.yml when it starts the `share` service.
#
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY bypasses Row-Level Security: it can read every trip
# in the project. It belongs in the deployment host's environment and nowhere
# else — never in a build arg, never in a commit, never in a chat.
# SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
#
# Reminders, sent by the same service. All optional: without VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY
# the service previews links and sends nothing.
#   VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY        the private half from generate-vapid-keys.mjs
#   VAPID_SUBJECT            mailto: the push services can write to
#   PUSH_WEBHOOK_SECRET      what a PostHog workflow presents on POST /push/send
#   PUSH_SEND_MODE           direct (default) or workflow
#   POSTHOG_KEY, POSTHOG_HOST  the same project key and host as the app
#   REMINDER_INTERVAL_SECONDS, REMINDER_EVE_HOUR_UTC, REMINDER_PICKUP_WINDOW_MINUTES
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:admin@kikouchou.app
# PUSH_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# PUSH_SEND_MODE=direct

# OAuth provider credentials — needed only to run `bun run db:config-push`.
#
# These are NOT read by the app. Nothing VITE_-prefixed means Vite never puts
# them in the browser bundle; they exist so `supabase config push` can resolve
# the `env(...)` references that `supabase/config.toml` uses for
# `[auth.external.*]`, and `scripts/supabase-config-push.js` reads them from
# .env.local so they need not be re-exported into every shell.
#
# Push without them and the CLI sends the literal string `env(NAME)` as the
# client ID, overwriting the credential in the hosted project — which is the
# only place it exists. That is the 2026-09-04 outage; the guard now refuses.
#
# Values come from the provider consoles: Google Cloud → APIs & Services →
# Credentials, and the Spotify developer dashboard.
# SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=xxxxxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
# SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_SECRET=GOCSPX-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_SPOTIFY_SECRET=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
